      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">{author.name}</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">{author.role}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#protein-sources" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Pea, Soy, Rice, Hemp &amp; Blends</a>
          <a href="#leucine-dose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Leucine and Dose</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#heavy-metals" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Heavy Metals</a>
          <a href="#cost-per-25g" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Protein and Cost per 25 g</a>
          <a href="#texture-sweeteners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Texture and Sweeteners</a>
          <a href="#how-much" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. How Much You Need</a>
          <a href="#plant-vs-whey" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Plant vs Whey</a>
          <a href="#timing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Timing and the Bigger Picture</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A good plant protein can build muscle about as well as whey, but only if you buy the right one and take enough of it. Most plant powders carry a little less leucine per gram than dairy, so the practical fix is a slightly bigger scoop: roughly 30-35 g of protein instead of 25 g. After dose, the two things that separate a good tub from a bad one are third-party testing, because plant proteins tend to carry more heavy metals, and an honest cost per 25 g of protein.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="protein-sources" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Pea, Soy, Rice, Hemp or a Blend: Which Source Is Complete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein is built from 20 amino acids, nine of which your body cannot make and must get from food. A protein is called complete when it supplies all nine in roughly the proportions your body needs. Almost every plant protein technically contains all nine; the real issue is that one or two come in low. That low one is called the limiting amino acid, and it caps how much of the protein your body can turn into new tissue.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pea</strong> is the base of most vegan powders for good reason. It is rich in lysine and arginine, mild in flavor once isolated, and cheap to produce at scale. Its weak spot is the sulfur amino acids, methionine and cysteine. <strong>Soy isolate</strong> is the closest plant match to dairy and scores at or near the top of the digestibility scales, but soy is one of the major food allergens and a lot of buyers avoid it, so fewer premium vegan powders use it now.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brown rice</strong> protein has the opposite profile to pea: lower in lysine, better in methionine. That is why pea and rice blends became the industry standard; each covers the other's gap. <strong>Hemp</strong> brings fiber, magnesium and some omega-3 fat, but hemp protein powders are often only around half protein by weight and run low in lysine, so they work better as a smoothie booster than as your main protein source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi-source blends go further. Garden of Life Sport combines pea with navy bean, lentil and garbanzo bean, while Vega Sport adds pumpkin and sunflower seed protein to pea. Single-ingredient pea is still a perfectly sound choice: at a 25-35 g dose, inside a diet that includes grains, nuts or soy foods, the methionine shortfall is small. The practical rule is to build on pea isolate, soy isolate or a multi-source blend, and not to rely on rice-only or hemp-only powders as your main shake when muscle is the goal.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Source</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main strength</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Limiting amino acid</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Leucine (approx. share of protein)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pea</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High lysine, mild flavor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Methionine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7-8%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soy isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Closest plant match to dairy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Methionine (minor)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7-8%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brown rice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Complements pea</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lysine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7-8%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hemp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fiber and omega-3 fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lysine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey (for reference)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest leucine density</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None meaningful</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10-12%</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="leucine-dose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Leucine and Dose: Why Plant Users Need a Slightly Bigger Scoop
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leucine is the amino acid that acts as the on switch for muscle protein synthesis. Research on the so-called leucine threshold suggests that a meal needs roughly 2-3 g of leucine to produce a near-maximal muscle-building response in young adults, with older adults sitting toward the top of that range. Total protein still matters, but leucine is the quickest way to see why plant and dairy powders are not interchangeable gram for gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The arithmetic is simple. Whey is roughly 10-12% leucine, so a 25 g scoop delivers somewhere around 2.5-3 g. Pea, soy and rice proteins sit nearer 7-8%, so the same 25 g gives you closer to 1.8-2 g, which is under the threshold. To reach about 2.5 g of leucine from a typical pea or pea-rice blend, you need roughly 30-35 g of protein. That is the whole case for a bigger plant scoop, and it costs you maybe 40-60 extra calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            This changes how you read a label. A product with 20-21 g of protein per serving, like Orgain Organic Protein or Momentous Essential Plant-Based, lands at roughly 1.5-1.7 g of leucine as sold. That is fine as a snack or alongside a meal, but after training you would want one and a half servings. Garden of Life Sport and Vega Sport list 30 g per serving, which crosses the threshold without any adjustment. Some brands also add free leucine or BCAAs; that helps, but check that the headline protein figure comes from intact protein rather than added amino acids.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dose matters more than source. In a 12-week trial led by Hevia-Larraín, vegans who supplemented with soy protein and omnivores who supplemented with whey were both brought to about 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day, and they gained muscle and strength at similar rates. The plant group did not need a special protein; it needed enough protein. Older lifters, who respond less to each meal, should lean toward 35-40 g of plant protein per shake.
          </p>

