        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests or a taste panel, and we will not pretend otherwise. These rankings come from label analysis, certification status and published research. For every candidate we recorded protein per serving, calories per serving, the protein sources used, the leucine-adjusted dose needed to match a 25 g whey scoop, the sweeteners and additives, independent testing, and cost per 25 g of protein at typical retail prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            Testing and protein density carried the most weight. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport exist mainly to screen for banned substances, which matters to drug-tested athletes, but the programs also verify that what is in the tub matches the label, and NSF's program screens for contaminants. For a category with known heavy-metal concerns, an independent auditor checking the product is worth paying something extra for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several things knocked products out. We skipped powders that hide protein amounts inside proprietary blends, premium-priced products with under 20 g of protein per serving, and greens or superfood powders marketed as protein when protein is a minority of the scoop. We also discounted labels where a big BCAA or amino acid claim could inflate the apparent protein content, a practice known as amino spiking that has hit the supplement industry before.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labels change. Brands reformulate, swap sweeteners and adjust scoop sizes, and certification listings come and go by product and flavor. Every figure on this page reflects the label at the time of writing. Before you buy, check the current tub, and if certification matters to you, search the exact product and flavor in the NSF or Informed Sport database rather than trusting a logo on an old product photo.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Heavy Metals in Plant Proteins: The Real Trade-Off
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plants pull lead, cadmium and arsenic out of the soil they grow in, and concentrating a crop down to its protein can concentrate some of those metals too. Independent testing published by Consumer Reports and by the nonprofit Clean Label Project has repeatedly found that plant-based protein powders, on average, carry more lead than dairy-based ones. Chocolate flavors tend to test higher again, because cacao carries lead and cadmium of its own, and rice protein draws particular attention for arsenic and cadmium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the scale in view. The amounts involved are usually measured in micrograms per serving, and the concern is cumulative exposure from daily use over years, not a single shake. The sensible response is a handful of free habits: choose a third-party tested product, prefer unflavored or vanilla over chocolate if you have more than one serving a day, and do not take three scoops a day of the same product indefinitely. A California Proposition 65 warning means a product exceeded a deliberately conservative threshold; it does not prove harm, but it is a data point.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people should be more careful than others: anyone who is pregnant or breastfeeding, children and teenagers, and people with kidney disease. If you fall into one of those groups, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before making a protein powder a daily habit. Our full guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers contaminants, testing programs and who should be cautious in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caveat on certifications: no seal guarantees zero heavy metals. Sport certifications are built around banned substances first, and contaminant limits differ between programs. A brand that publishes lot-level certificates of analysis, including metals, earns extra credit with us, because it lets you check the batch you are actually drinking rather than trusting a general claim.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Shortcut</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tested, lightly flavored, one or two scoops a day</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">You do not need to fear plant protein, but you should not be casual about it either. A third-party tested powder, unflavored or vanilla rather than chocolate, used once or twice a day as part of a mixed diet, keeps your exposure low without giving up the benefits.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

